From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 16:27:07 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] signal: the fault-loop breaker does not count a breakpoint

FEX raises every guest int3 from one BRK in its code, so the Mach
handler sees each at the same pc and address. World of Warcraft's x64
loader takes int3 after int3 on its main thread through its own
exception handlers; the breaker counted them as one pc faulting 50 times
and ended the thread (run 20261101T161719: [fault-stuck] BREAKING LOOP,
then the client hung with no window).

A breakpoint is an exception delivered to the guest, as on Windows, not
a fault nothing resolves: like an alignment fault, it is not counted.

Linear: PLA-516, PLA-514
Class: ios-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-11-01-wow-x64-fex.md
Offered-upstream: no; the breaker is Playport's own
---
 build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c b/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c
index 398b913..51d4c67 100644
--- a/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c
+++ b/build/ntdll-unix/signal_arm64_ios.c
@@ -4831,12 +4831,19 @@ skip_reclaim_band: ;
                          * pages). ml385: NULL-page faults keyed as 1. PLA-238:
                          * alignment faults (EXC_ARM_DA_ALIGN, misaligned atomics FEX
                          * emulates) are not counted. PLA-514: keyed on the thread
-                         * too; pp_fault_loop.h has the rules. */
+                         * too; pp_fault_loop.h has the rules. PLA-516: neither is a
+                         * breakpoint (EXC_BREAKPOINT): FEX raises every guest int3
+                         * from one BRK in its code, so a client whose handlers take
+                         * int3 after int3 on one thread (World of Warcraft's x64
+                         * loader) looked like one pc faulting 50 times, and its main
+                         * thread was ended. Each is an exception delivered to the
+                         * guest, as on Windows, never a fault nothing resolves. */
                         static volatile struct ppfl_state fault_loop;
                         uint64_t fpage = ppfl_page( (uint64_t)fault_addr );
                         uint32_t pn;
                         uint32_t n = ppfl_note( &fault_loop, thread, fault_pc_check, (uint64_t)fault_addr,
-                                                fault_kr == EXC_ARM_DA_ALIGN, &pn );
+                                                fault_kr == EXC_ARM_DA_ALIGN || req->exception == EXC_BREAKPOINT,
+                                                &pn );
                         if (pn && (pn % 1000) == 0)
                             dprintf(STDERR_FILENO,
                                 "[fault-stuck] ml876 page 0x%llx pc 0x%llx: %u faults, addresses advancing (last 0x%llx)\n",
-- 
